{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And now, etc.</b> It may be explained according to the saying (Genesis Rabbah 21:6): ״וְעַתָּה״ (and now) means nothing other than repentance — and this is what Moses pleaded before HaShem: that Israel had repented of the evil thing." "<b>If You will bear their sin.</b> Meaning, it is preferable. One needs to know why he did not finish his words. Or perhaps the measure of his words follows this path: <b>And now</b> — meaning, behold, Israel have returned in repentance, and this addresses what depends on them; but as for what depends on Him, blessed be He, it is unknown whether HaShem will accept their repentance for the great sin or not. Therefore he concluded by saying, <b>If You will bear their sin</b> — for repentance requires the one who returns and the one who accepts." "<b>And if not, blot me out, etc.</b> Meaning, because through the intermediacy of all that transpired between Israel and HaShem blessed be He, the faithful intercessor merits merit that accrues to him from every commandment — and especially at the giving of the Torah, how much merit accrued to Moses through his exertions together with Israel, all of it written in a book of remembrance before Him. From this it follows that if He does not bear their sin, it is necessary to blot from the book all of Moses' merits that were recorded through the merit of Israel. And HaShem answered him that his words are not true in this matter, for it is precisely the sinner himself whom HaShem will blot from His book after he has been written in the book; but one who merited through his intermediacy — his merit which he earned will not be voided on account of the other's having turned back and wickedly sinned." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the words of those of blessed memory (Zohar vol. 3, 193a), who said that all the souls of the wilderness generation were branches of the soul of Moses. From this it follows that when He does not bear their sin, behold, the deficiency touches Moses himself; therefore he said, <b>blot me out, etc.</b> And HaShem answered that the branches cause harm only to themselves and not to the aspect of the root of the soul — and this is what He said: <b>Whoever has sinned against Me</b> — precisely he is the one <b>whom I will blot from My book</b> — but the aspect of Moses' soul, no." "<b>Further,</b> it intends: if He does not bear their sin, behold, he too has sinned many times before Him, as we find that he said (Exodus 5:22): ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה״ and the like — and if so, his sin too will not be borne and he needs to be blotted, etc., heaven forbid. And the Creator answered: <b>Whoever has sinned against Me</b> — meaning, that his sin is different from the sin of idolatry, by which they denied the Elohim of Israel and obscured the divinity as they were commanded; for the act of the intermediary, relative to Israel, falls under the category of uprooting everything — and this is what He was precise in saying: <b>sinned against Me</b> — unlike other sins, which are forgiven through repentance without punishment." "Now the entirety of Moses' argument with HaShem is only that He forgive them immediately, and that they be written in the book of the righteous; but not regarding the matter that they have hope after reproofs for sin, for this is self-evident, that it will be atoned. And this is what he was precise in saying, <b>from Your book that You have written</b> — meaning, for three books are written on high: one of the righteous, etc., and one of the wicked, and one of the intermediate, and they are opened on Rosh HaShanah. The book that is of the righteous — the Holy One, blessed be He, writes it and sets it before Him, and this is what he said, <b>from Your book</b>, meaning that one which You have written Yourself, not those that are written through others. And he needed to explain <b>that You have written</b>, because from the designation <b>from Your book</b> alone it is not understood that it refers to the book of the righteous, for everything belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He, for He is the Master of all." "And in tractate Rosh HaShanah (Rosh HaShanah 16b) they expounded the verse in this manner: ״מִסִּפְרְךָ״ — this is the book of the righteous; ״אֲשֶׁר כָּתָבְתָּ״ — this is the book of the intermediate. End quote. And according to their words, may their memory be for blessing, it is explained based on their saying, may their memory be for blessing (Kiddushin 40b), that a person should always regard himself as though half of him is meritorious and half of him is liable, and likewise they said elsewhere (Berakhot 61b) that a person should not assess himself except as an intermediate. And from this the words of Moses are explained in this manner: for Moses assessed himself as an intermediate, according to the law of every man of Israel, and therefore he said that if He does not bear the sin of Israel, his merits would be diminished — and not merely from the assessment of a righteous person, but even from that of an intermediate, as he said, ״אֲשֶׁר כָּתָבְתָּ״." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that, given the intermediacy of Israel — if they act righteously, he would stand alive in the degree of a righteous person, for his merits are many. And if He does not bear their transgression, not only would he not gain additional merits to tip his merits [in his favor] so as to be called righteous, but on the contrary, his earlier merit would be counted to him as a diminution when they are punished, for it would have been better had there not been any of the good that preceded. And understand this." "<b>Further</b>, the faithful shepherd intends to say against that which had preceded him in the utterance of HaShem, ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״, and he said before Him that if HaShem does not wish to bear their sin, he chooses that He erase him from His book that He wrote — meaning, for at the time that He said to him, ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״, it is surely written in the book of the upright from the moment it issued from the mouth of El Elyon, and must of necessity be fulfilled — for even regarding a curse they said, may their memory be for blessing (Makkot 11a), that a sage's curse, even on a condition, [stands] — and all the more so a good matter that issues from the mouth of the Most High, it is written for a witness and a sign. And therefore he prays that if He does not forgive Israel, He should erase him, and thereby there is no room to fulfill the oath to the patriarchs. And HaShem answered that it will not arise and will not be — that He should erase him — but rather, etc.; but the good that He spoke concerning Moses will stand forever. And so it was, as it is written (1 Chronicles 23:17) ״וּבְנֵי רְחַבְיָה רָבוּ לְמַעְלָה״, and they said, may their memory be for blessing (Berakhot 7a): לְמַעְלָה — above sixty myriads."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {8 "Makkot 11a: the Or HaChaim cites the principle of a sage's curse even on condition; the exact page reference (יא,א) likely refers to folio 11a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:41.236530469Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}